<title>SOUTH KOREA: South Korea's Q1 energy demand rises 2.0 pct.</title>
<headline>South Korea's Q1 energy demand rises 2.0 pct.</headline>
<dateline>SEOUL 1997-05-16</dateline>
<text>
<p>South Korea's energy demand rose 2.0 percent to 45.43 million tonnes of oil equivalent (TOE) in the first quarter of 1997 from 44.56 million a year ago, the Ministry of Trade, Industry and Energy said on Friday.</p>
<p>In comparison, demand gained 12.1 percent in the first quarter of 1996, a ministry statement said.</p>
<p>Energy consumption growth slowed due to the industrial downturn, rising energy prices and warmer weather, it said.</p>
<p>The breakdown of South Korea's demand for energy resources in the first quarter, compared with the same period last year (Figures in brackets are year-on-year percentage growth):  </p>
<p>				 Q1 1997		  Q1 1996</p>
<p>				 Amount		    Amount</p>
<p> Total			45,429   (2.0)	44,555  (12.1)</p>
<p>(in 1,000 TOE)</p>
<p> Petroleum products    203,963  (-0.4)     204,840   (9.6)</p>
<p>(in 1,000 barrels)</p>
<p> LNG			   3,718  (36.3)	 2,727  (28.4)</p>
<p>(in 1,000 tonnes)</p>
<p> Hard coal		     920 (-20.4)	 1,156 (-17.3)</p>
<p>(in 1,000 tonnes)</p>
<p> Bituminous coal	  11,331   (3.7)	10,926  (21.1)</p>
<p>(in 1,000 tonnes)</p>
<p> Nuclear power	    15,884  (-9.8)	17,608   (9.8)</p>
<p>(in gigawatt hours)</p>
<p> Hydroelectric power	 975  (-9.9)	 1,082  (12.5)</p>
<p>(in gigawatt hours)</p>
<p> Other			   311  (14.6)	   272   (7.5)</p>
<p>(in 1,000 TOE)</p>
<p>(Source: Ministry of Trade, Industry and Energy).</p>
<p>--Seoul Newsroom (822) 727 5643</p>
</text>
